# Researcher

Before the analyst can draft a single line of outreach, somebody has to *know
something* about the account. Who are they, what changed recently, why would
they care about us this quarter? That is research, and on this workbook it is
not a human chore — it is an agent. This file builds the **auto-researcher**: a
brain that takes a ranked [[Lead]] and produces a short, sourced dossier by
running a loop — **plan, search, verify, synthesize** — until it has enough to
hand off.

An auto-researcher is the second agent shape this workbook teaches (the first,
the ReAct coding agent, lives in [[Coder]]). Where ReAct interleaves thought and
action freely, the auto-researcher has a **fixed shape**: it decides *what it
needs to find out*, goes and finds it, checks that what it found is actually
true, and only then writes it up. The discipline is the point. A research loop
that skips the verify step is just a confident hallucinator, and a sales dossier
built on a hallucination is worse than no dossier at all.

## Where it runs and why

The agent's loop runs on the **server**, the nexus tier. That is not a stylistic
choice. The loop needs the network to search, it needs to call an LLM with a
real key, and it keeps memory across iterations — all three are server
capabilities. The browser never sees the key and never makes the search calls;
it only renders the finished dossier. So the agent runs on the **server** — its
loop has a real body that runs on the nexus, exactly like the production
agents in the runtime. (Contrast a pure *definition*, which has no body to run
and so takes no placement at all.)

A brain is three things: a **system prompt** (who it is), a **model** (what
thinks), and **tools** (what it can do). Tools are the only way the brain
touches the world, and each tool is a grant the [[Dock]] fulfils. Here it gets
exactly two: web search, and a fetch-and-read. Nothing else — no filesystem, no
shell. A researcher that cannot write files cannot wreck anything.

We isolate the whole thing in a named sandbox so those two grants are the
*only* capabilities the loop ever holds. The sandbox profile is a plain module
attribute, reused so the policy lives in one place:

@research grant: [:net], memory: "256mb", timeout: 120_000

The `:net` grant is scoped at the tool boundary to the search and fetch
endpoints; the brain cannot open arbitrary sockets just because it can search.
Caps flow by grant, not by inheritance — nesting a tool inside this sandbox does
not hand it the model key unless the key is granted too.

## The dossier it produces

The loop's output is a typed value, not loose prose. We give it a `Dossier`
struct so the analyst downstream gets a stable shape to read, and so a half-done
dossier (no findings yet, not verified) is structurally distinct from a finished
one. Field types are inferred from the defaults — a string stays a string, a
list stays a list, `verified?` is a boolean.

defmodule Dossier do
  defstruct account: "",
            domain: "",
            summary: "",
            findings: [],
            sources: [],
            verified?: false
end

A single finding is just a tagged tuple — a claim paired with the source URL it
came from. Keeping the source welded to the claim is what makes the verify step
possible at all: `{:fact, "Initech raised a Series B in March", "https://…"}`.
The variant `{:ok, %Dossier{}} | {:error, reason}` is what the loop ultimately
returns, so a caller can pattern-match success from a dead end.

## The brain

Here is the agent itself. The system prompt fixes the loop's discipline; the
model and tools fix its reach. Everything below `sandbox :research` is inside the
two-grant boundary.

sandbox :research, @research do
  agent :researcher do
    @moduledoc """
    Auto-researcher. Given a ranked %Lead{}, produce a sourced %Dossier{} by
    looping plan -> search -> verify -> synthesize. Output is typed and every
    finding carries its source. Never invents a fact it cannot cite.
    """

    model "minimax/minimax-m3"

    system """
    You research B2B sales accounts. You work in a strict loop:

      1. PLAN     — list the 3-5 questions whose answers would actually change
                    how we sell to this account (funding, headcount, tech
                    stack, recent news, a relevant pain).
      2. SEARCH   — for each open question, search the web. Read the most
                    promising result in full before believing it.
      3. VERIFY   — never keep a claim you cannot tie to a specific source.
                    If two sources disagree, prefer the primary one and note
                    the conflict. Drop anything you cannot stand behind.
      4. SYNTHESIZE — once the questions are answered, write a tight summary
                    and a short list of cited findings. Stop.

    You may NOT invent revenue, names, or events. A missing answer is a fine
    outcome; a fabricated one is a failure. Cite every finding.
    """

    # The only two doors to the world. Each is a granted capability the Dock
    # fulfils over :net — no filesystem, no shell, no model key leaked to them.
    tool :search, "search the web; returns ranked {title, url, snippet}" do
      query -> Search.web(query, limit: 6)
    end

    tool :read, "fetch a url and return clean readable text" do
      url -> Fetch.readable(url)
    end
  end
end

## The loop as a flow

The brain *can* loop on its own, but on this workbook we make the loop
**explicit** as a flow so it is inspectable and testable — you can see each
stage in the code graph and a red test on `verify` ships no bundle. The flow
drives the brain stage by stage, threading the growing `Dossier` through. This
is the same `step`/`from:` orchestration [[Pipeline]] uses, now wrapped around an
agent instead of pure compute.

flow :research_account do
  @moduledoc """
  Run the auto-researcher loop over one lead and return {:ok, %Dossier{}}.
  plan -> search -> verify -> synthesize, looped until the plan is answered
  or the iteration budget runs out.
  """

  # Stage 1 — PLAN. Ask the brain for the handful of questions worth chasing.
  # We seed a fresh Dossier from the lead so every later stage has a typed home
  # to write into.
  step :plan, from: :lead do
    dossier = %Dossier{account: lead.name, domain: lead.domain}
    questions = Researcher.plan(lead)
    {dossier, questions}
  end

  # Stage 2 — SEARCH. For each open question, the brain searches and reads.
  # The questions are independent, so we fan out — same parallel-for-independence
  # rule as the enrich stage in [[Pipeline]]. Each task comes back with raw
  # candidate findings still tagged with their source url.
  step :search, from: :plan do
    {dossier, questions} = plan
    candidates =
      parallel questions, max_concurrency: 4, timeout: 60_000 do
        question -> Researcher.investigate(question)
      end
    {dossier, List.flatten(candidates)}
  end

  # Stage 3 — VERIFY. The stage that earns the agent its keep. We re-read each
  # candidate's source and keep only the claims the source actually supports.
  # Anything unsupported is dropped, not softened. The surviving findings are
  # what the dossier is allowed to assert.
  step :verify, from: :search do
    {dossier, candidates} = search
    findings =
      Enum.filter(candidates, fn {:fact, claim, url} ->
        Researcher.supported?(claim, url)
      end)
    sources = findings |> Enum.map(fn {:fact, _, url} -> url end) |> Enum.uniq()
    {%Dossier{dossier | findings: findings, sources: sources}, candidates}
  end

  # Stage 4 — SYNTHESIZE. Hand the brain the verified findings and ask for a
  # tight summary. We stamp verified? true only once we are here with real,
  # source-backed findings — never before.
  step :synthesize, from: :verify do
    {dossier, _} = verify
    summary = Researcher.write_summary(dossier)
    {:ok, %Dossier{dossier | summary: summary, verified?: true}}
  end
end

## Why the verify stage is non-negotiable

It is tempting to collapse `:search` and `:synthesize` and let the model write
straight from its search results. Do not. The whole reason an *auto*-researcher
beats a one-shot prompt is the explicit `:verify` checkpoint: it re-opens each
source and confirms the source truly says what the candidate finding claims.
Claims that survive go in; claims that do not are dropped on the floor. That is
the difference between a dossier the analyst can quote in front of a prospect and
a generator of plausible nonsense.

This also makes the agent **measurable**. Because every finding is a
`{:fact, claim, url}` tuple and `verified?` only flips true after the filter, a
test can assert that no finding in a finished dossier lacks a source, and that an
unverified dossier never reaches the analyst. That assertion runs at the weave
gate — a researcher that emits an uncited fact fails the build.

## Handoff

A finished dossier flows straight into the rest of the workbook. The analyst
agent reads `dossier.summary` and `dossier.findings` to ground its outreach
draft, so the email it writes cites something real instead of guessing. The
[[Plan]] gets a `%Task{title: "Research Initech", status: :done, owner: nil,
ref: dossier}` once the loop returns `{:ok, _}`, closing the loop between
"we should look into this account" and "here is what we found".

case Research.run(lead) do
  {:ok, dossier} ->
    publish(:dossier, dossier)
    Board.complete("Research #{lead.name}", ref: dossier)

  {:error, reason} ->
    # A dead end is a real outcome — record it, do not fabricate a dossier.
    Board.flag("Research #{lead.name}", reason)
end

The auto-researcher is the same one authoring model as every other unit here: a
brain is a module, its tools are grants, its loop is a flow, and its output is a
typed struct. Learn the loop — plan, search, verify, synthesize — and you have
learned the shape every research agent on the platform runs.
